On Windows, the easiest way to do this is using the built-in Set-Clipboard command (or its shorter alias, scb).
## 1. Using Pipes (Most Common)
If you want to send the output of a command directly to your clipboard:

echo "Hello from the terminal" | Set-Clipboard

Or using the alias:

ipconfig | scb

## 2. Passing a Specific String
If you just want to put a specific piece of text onto the clipboard without echoing it first:

Set-Clipboard -Value "This text is now copied"

## 3. Copying the Contents of a File
If you have a log file or a config file and want the whole thing on your clipboard:

Get-Content .\myfile.txt | Set-Clipboard

## 4. The "Old School" Way (Works in CMD too)
If you are in a regular Command Prompt or just prefer the classic tool, Windows has had clip for decades:

echo "Legacy copy" | clip

## Pro Tip: Appending Text
Unlike the old clip tool, the PowerShell Set-Clipboard command can append text to what is already there instead of overwriting it:

Set-Clipboard -Value "New line of text" -Append

